(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-1-ans-adrenergic-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Adrenergic Drugs Quiz 2 — Pharmacology I Exam 1","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Why is caution advised with dobutamine in atrial fibrillation?","choices":["It slows sinoatrial firing","It causes bronchoconstriction","It depletes cardiac catecholamine stores","It increases atrioventricular conduction"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, which can accelerate the ventricular response. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 94"},{"q":"How do the indirect-acting adrenergic agonists work?","choices":["They bind postsynaptic receptors and mimic norepinephrine","They inhibit catechol-O-methyltransferase","They increase synthesis of norepinephrine from tyrosine","They release norepinephrine, or block its reuptake"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and they do not act on postsynaptic receptors directly. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 99"},{"q":"Why does norepinephrine cause reflex bradycardia?","choices":["It stimulates cardiac muscarinic receptors directly","It blocks beta-one receptors in the heart","It depletes cardiac norepinephrine stores","A baroreceptor reflex raises vagal activity"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and atropine pretreatment blocks that vagal limb so tachycardia appears instead. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 89"},{"q":"Why do the noncatecholamines have longer half-lives?","choices":["They are excreted unchanged by the kidney","They are not substrates for the inactivating enzymes","They are protein bound and released slowly from plasma","They are stored in vesicles and released over hours"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and their greater lipid solubility also increases central penetration. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"What is propranolol used for beyond hypertension?","choices":["Migraine, hyperthyroidism, angina and infarction","Glaucoma, asthma and benign prostatic hyperplasia","Shock, cardiac arrest and anaphylaxis","Pheochromocytoma and clonidine withdrawal"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and after infarction it reduces infarct size and sudden death. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 116"},{"q":"Besides blocking norepinephrine reuptake, what else does cocaine do?","choices":["It depletes vesicular stores of norepinephrine","It stimulates dopaminergic receptors in the kidney","It blocks neuronal sodium channels","It inhibits monoamine oxidase"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and it also blocks sodium-potassium activated ATPase. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 102"},{"q":"Which forms of shock is dopamine used for?","choices":["Hypovolaemic only","Cardiogenic and septic","Anaphylactic and haemorrhagic","Neurogenic only"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 93"},{"q":"Where are adrenergic receptors located relative to the synapse?","choices":["Presynaptically or postsynaptically","Only postsynaptically on the effector organ","Only presynaptically on the neuron","Only within the central nervous system"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and that presynaptic location is what alpha-two agonists exploit. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"What are albuterol's adverse effects?","choices":["Sedation, dry mouth and constipation","Bradycardia and bronchoconstriction","Miosis, salivation and diarrhoea","Tremor, restlessness and anxiety"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, the familiar beta-two agonist profile. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 98"},{"q":"Which vascular beds does dopamine dilate, and through which receptors?","choices":["Skeletal muscle beds, through alpha-two receptors","Pulmonary vessels, through dopaminergic receptors","Renal and splanchnic, through dopaminergic receptors","Coronary and cerebral, through beta-two receptors"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, which is distinct from its beta-one cardiac and high-dose alpha-one effects. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 92"},{"q":"Which vascular effect does propranolol produce, and through which receptor?","choices":["Renal vasodilation, through dopaminergic receptors","Peripheral vasoconstriction, through beta-two blockade","Peripheral vasodilation, through alpha-one blockade","No vascular effect at all"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, since blocking beta-two removes vasodilation. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 115"},{"q":"Catecholamines barely enter the brain, yet cause central-type effects. Which?","choices":["Hallucinations, delirium and seizures","Anxiety, tremor and headache","Sedation, lethargy and constipation","Depression, nightmares and memory loss"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. They are described as CNS-like effects despite the drugs being too polar to enter the brain. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"What is intravenous labetalol used for?","choices":["Hypertensive emergencies","Chronic open-angle glaucoma","Acute asthma","Septic shock"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, using its combined alpha and beta blockade. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 122"},{"q":"Which receptor does dobutamine selectively stimulate?","choices":["Alpha-one","Alpha-two","Beta-one","Beta-two"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, a synthetic selective agonist. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 94"},{"q":"What happens to cardioselectivity at higher doses?","choices":["It is lost","It increases","It is unchanged","It converts to alpha blockade"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, so a beta-one selective agent can still affect the airway. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 120"},{"q":"What is the rank order of potency at beta receptors?","choices":["Isoproterenol, then epinephrine, then norepinephrine","Epinephrine, then norepinephrine, then isoproterenol","Norepinephrine, then epinephrine, then isoproterenol","All three are equipotent at beta receptors"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The alpha order runs the other way, with epinephrine and norepinephrine far above isoproterenol. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"What is timolol's ocular action, and what is it used for?","choices":["It reduces aqueous humour production","It causes miosis, for chronic open-angle glaucoma","It causes mydriasis, for examination of the fundus","It reduces corneal oedema, for keratitis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, a different route to lowering pressure than pilocarpine's. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 119"},{"q":"Through which receptor does epinephrine produce bronchodilation?","choices":["Alpha-two","Beta-two","Beta-one","Alpha-one"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, the same receptor albuterol targets selectively. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 84"},{"q":"In which patients is propranolol contraindicated?","choices":["Those with hypertension","Those with migraine","Those recovering from myocardial infarction","Those with asthma or chronic lung disease"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, because beta-two blockade causes bronchoconstriction. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 115"},{"q":"At what dose does dopamine begin to cause vasoconstriction, and through which receptor?","choices":["At low doses, through beta-two","At any dose, through dopaminergic receptors","At very high doses, through alpha-one","At low doses, through alpha-one"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, whereas its cardiac effect is beta-one and its renal effect dopaminergic. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 92"},{"q":"What distinguishes a cardioselective beta blocker from a nonselective one?","choices":["It has intrinsic sympathomimetic activity","It blocks beta-one but spares beta-two","It blocks beta-two receptors rather than beta-one","It blocks alpha-one in addition to beta receptors"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, though cardioselectivity is lost at higher doses. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 113"},{"q":"Why does a local anaesthetic solution often contain epinephrine?","choices":["It provides additional analgesia of its own","It prevents an allergic reaction to the anaesthetic","It causes vasoconstriction at the injection site","It speeds systemic absorption of the anaesthetic"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and applied topically it also helps control capillary oozing. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 85"},{"q":"How does guanethidine act?","choices":["It blocks release of stored norepinephrine","It blocks transport of transmitters into storage vesicles","It blocks postsynaptic alpha-one receptors","It stimulates presynaptic alpha-two receptors"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, which is distinct from reserpine's blockade of vesicular uptake. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 123"},{"q":"What is phenylephrine used for?","choices":["Nasal decongestant, eye redness and septic shock","Asthma and chronic obstructive pulmonary disease","Hypertension and opiate withdrawal","Glaucoma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 96"},{"q":"Which effect do the alpha-adrenergic blocking agents share?","choices":["Bronchoconstriction","Reduced aqueous humour production","Reflex tachycardia","Reflex bradycardia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and their effects on blood pressure are more profound when the patient stands. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 107"},{"q":"Why is epinephrine ineffective if swallowed?","choices":["It is destroyed by gastric acid before absorption","It is inactivated by intestinal enzymes","It is not absorbed across the gut wall at all","It binds irreversibly to gastric mucosa"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, which is why it is given by injection, endotracheal tube or inhalation. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 86"},{"q":"What are epinephrine's emergency indications?","choices":["Cardiogenic and septic shock","Hypertensive emergency","Chronic heart failure","Anaphylactic shock and cardiac arrest"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, alongside acute reversal of bronchospasm. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 85"},{"q":"Which tumour do both phenoxybenzamine and phentolamine treat?","choices":["Insulinoma","Pheochromocytoma","Carcinoid tumour","Small cell lung carcinoma"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, a catecholamine-secreting tumour. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 109"},{"q":"Which receptors do labetalol and carvedilol block?","choices":["Alpha-one and alpha-two only","Beta-two only","Alpha-one, beta-one and beta-two","Beta-one only"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and the alpha-one component is what produces peripheral vasodilation. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 122"},{"q":"Which two agents have intrinsic sympathomimetic activity?","choices":["Atenolol and metoprolol","Timolol and nadolol","Labetalol and carvedilol","Acebutolol and pindolol"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: they weakly stimulate beta-one and beta-two while blocking. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 121"}]};
